STRTOLL(3)                     P32W Unix                      STRTOLL(3)

NAME
       strtoll, strtoull, atoll, llabs - convert a string to a long long

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       long long strtoll(const char *s, char **end, int base);
       unsigned long long strtoull(const char *s, char **end, int base);
       long long atoll(const char *s);
       long long llabs(long long v);

DESCRIPTION
       strtoll and strtoull are strtol and strtoul (see atoi(3)) in
       64 bits: the same white space, sign, bases (2 to 36, or 0
       for C's prefixes) and *end. A value too large gives ERANGE
       and the nearest limit: LLONG_MIN or LLONG_MAX for strtoll,
       ULLONG_MAX for strtoull (limits.h).

       atoll(s) is strtoll(s, NULL, 10). llabs is the absolute
       value of a long long.

RETURN VALUE
       The value converted; 0 if there were no digits; the limit
       on overflow.

EXAMPLES
       long long n = strtoll(argv[1], NULL, 0);
       printf("%lld\n", n * 1000000);

SEE ALSO
       atoi(3), printf(3), scanf(3), stdlib(3)

BUGS
       A long long is kept in memory and its arithmetic is done by
       library calls (see cc(1)), so it is many times slower than
       int. scanf has no %lld.
